== Normalized definitions ==

=== Machine learning ===
Machine learning studies computational methods that learn patterns, representations, policies, or decision rules from data or experience.

=== Generalization (machine learning) ===
Generalization is the ability of a learned system to perform usefully on new examples or conditions beyond the specific examples used to fit it.

=== Overfitting ===
Overfitting occurs when adaptation to finite-sample details yields misleadingly strong fit on development data relative to unseen target performance.

=== Underfitting ===
Underfitting occurs when the learned predictor fails to capture relevant structure needed for good task performance.

=== Inductive bias ===
Inductive bias is the set of assumptions, preferences, constraints, or prior structure that enables a learner to choose among data-compatible hypotheses and predict beyond observed examples.

=== Bias–variance tradeoff ===
Bias–variance analysis decomposes expected prediction error into systematic error, sensitivity to training-sample variation, and irreducible noise under specified assumptions.

=== Training set ===
A training set is the data used directly to fit model parameters or representations.

=== Validation set ===
A validation set is data excluded from direct parameter fitting and used during development for model/hyperparameter/design selection.

=== Test set ===
A test set is held out from development and used to estimate performance after choices are fixed.

=== Distribution shift ===
Distribution shift occurs when the data-generating distribution relevant at evaluation or deployment differs from the training distribution.

=== Data leakage (machine learning) ===
Machine-learning data leakage occurs when information unavailable under intended prediction/evaluation conditions improperly influences fitting, preprocessing, validation, or selection.

=== Benchmark contamination ===
Benchmark contamination occurs when benchmark items, answers, derivatives, or benchmark-specific information influence training/tuning so benchmark scores cease to provide clean independent evidence.

=== Model selection bias ===
Model selection bias occurs when choosing the best-looking model/configuration on a noisy finite-sample selection criterion causes the selected score to overestimate future performance.

=== Spurious correlation ===
A spurious correlation is an observed association that does not represent the stable task-relevant relationship intended for prediction and can break under changed conditions.

=== Shortcut learning ===
Shortcut learning occurs when a learner exploits comparatively easy predictive cues that succeed on the benchmark/training distribution but fail to capture the intended robust decision strategy.

