== Modern-learning correction ==

Classical capacity narratives are retained only with scope.

A simple textbook picture:

<pre>
too simple → underfit
moderate complexity → best test error
too complex → overfit
</pre>

is useful in restricted settings but is not a universal law.

Modern overparameterized models can interpolate training data and still generalize; double-descent behavior can violate the single-U-curve story.

Therefore Core/Technical pages must distinguish classical bias–variance decomposition from empirical complexity curves.

== Dataset-role correction ==

Training / validation / test are functional roles, not fixed percentages.

Cross-validation may rotate which observations serve validation roles.

Nested cross-validation can separate model selection from final performance estimation.

Repeatedly consulting a test set converts it into part of the development process.

== Leakage / contamination correction ==

<pre>
data leakage
 ├→ target leakage
 ├→ temporal leakage
 ├→ preprocessing leakage
 ├→ group/duplicate overlap
 └→ train–test contamination
       └→ benchmark contamination (evaluation-specific case)
</pre>

Benchmark contamination can also occur indirectly through derivative data, benchmark-specific tuning, or synthetic pipelines.

Not every benchmark flaw is contamination.

== Distribution / spurious / shortcut correction ==

A model can fail under shift even without ordinary overfitting.

A spurious correlation is a relation in the data/modeling context.

Shortcut learning is the learner's use of an easy cue/decision rule.

The two often interact but are not synonymous.

